  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Four independent choices",
      "items": [
        "Topology: who owns the authoritative simulation",
        "Driver store: which driver instances exist at all",
        "Interface: how bytes leave the process, in-process or socket",
        "Reachability: whether the client can address the host directly"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "What the play type decides",
      "items": [
        "Client and server: both worlds, a listen server, the default",
        "Client only: one world, must connect to something",
        "Server only: one world, can only listen",
        "There is no separate client-hosted mode, only client and server on a player machine"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Not in these packages",
      "items": [
        "There is no hole punching or address translation traversal anywhere in transport",
        "Relay allocation, join codes, matchmaking and lobbies are all service-side",
        "Fleet and server hosting is a Unity service, documented outside this book",
        "Distributed authority does not exist in netcode for entities today"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Choosing",
      "items": [
        "Cheating matters and budget allows: a dedicated server",
        "Friends, small sessions, low cost: a listen server behind a relay",
        "Browser client: it cannot listen, so it must be a client",
        "Develop with the simulator on, which forces sockets and exposes real bugs"
      ]
    }
  ]
